#{[17592186119621 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(B),$ we need to check if the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[B \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[B\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 15 & -6 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & \\leftrightarrow {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}5 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the true statement $0=0.$ So the system is equivalent to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=0.$ Substituting into the first equation gives $x_1=\\dfrac 7 5.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(B).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\n\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}7 \\\\ 21 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{b}} = \\dfrac{7}{5} \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{b}_1} + 0 \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 6 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{b}_2}\n$$"] [17592186119622 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $C\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[C\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3=1$. Substituting into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}+4(1) & =1 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} & =-3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5-(-1)-4(1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $C\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{c}_1,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\n\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ - 4 \\\\ 4\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{b}} =  2 \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{c}_1} + (-1) \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{c}_2}+ 1 \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{c}_3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get $x_1 \\cdot x_2 \\cdot x_3 = 2 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot 1 = -2$."] [17592186119670 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ then the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2+k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2+k\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -32 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k-48\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -11 & k-48\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+11{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-4\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=10 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 0 &=k-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-4,$ which means that $k=4.$"] [17592186120313 :question/problem "The matrix $B$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $b ∈ Col(B)$. In the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}$, and ${\\mathbf{b}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{b}}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $B$.\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186120314 :question/problem "The matrix $C$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b ∈ Col(C)$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2} \\cdot x_{3}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}$ and $x_{3}$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{c}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{c}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{c}}_{\\mathbf{3}}$, and ${\\mathbf{c}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{c}}_{2}$, and ${\\mathbf{c}}_{\\mathbf{3}}$ are the respective columns of $C$.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186120339 :question/problem "The matrix $H$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Col}(H).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{h}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{h}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{h}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{h}}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $H.$\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186120397 :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 \\\\ -2 & -11 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -15 \\\\ k+5\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$"] [17592186120398 :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $Col(A)$."] [17592186120515 :question/problem "The matrix $E$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below.\nDetermine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Col}(E).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{e}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{e}}_{\\mathbf{2}}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{e}}_{\\mathbf{3}}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{e}}_{\\mathbf{4}},$ and ${\\mathbf{e}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{e}}_{2}$, ${\\mathbf{e}}_{3}$, and ${\\mathbf{e}}_{4}$ are respective columns of $E.$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -16 & 28 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186120515 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $E\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[E\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 7 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -16 & 28 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & -4 & 7 & 1 & 2 \\\\ \\text{ }0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} &=2 \\\\ 0 &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-1.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$"] [17592186247182 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-2 & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-2 \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-1 & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186247182 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 & 2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & \\,\\,-1 & 2\\end{array}\\right] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=-6 \\\\ 0 &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=1.$ \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Col}(A).$"] [17592186247183 :question/problem "The matrix $M$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Col}(M).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{m}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{m}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{m}}_{1},$  ${\\mathbf{m}}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $M.$ \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186247183 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Col}(M),$ we need to check if the equation $M\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[M\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[M\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2} &=8 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=6.$  \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $M\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Col}(M).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{m}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{m}}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{b}}=6\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{m}}_{\\mathbf{1}}}+(-2)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{m}}_{\\mathbf{2}}}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=6(-2)=-12.$"] [17592186247185 :question/problem "The matrix $C$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ are given below. Determine if $\\mathbf{b}\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $\\mathbf{b}={x}_{1}{\\mathbf{c}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{c}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{c}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{c}}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $C.$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186247185 :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $C\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}[C\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-1 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=9 \\\\ 0 &=12.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get the false statement $0=12.$  \n\nTherefore, the system is inconsistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b}\\notin \\text{Col}(C).$"] [17592186247187 :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$"] [17592186345152 :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{x}}=\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\hspace{0.222em})}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}}+\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}5\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=-2\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+(-1)\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+5\\cdot {\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{3}=5.$"] [17592186345154 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186345154 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ 0 &=1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere the third equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that $\\mathbf{x}\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\}.$"] [17592186345167 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2},{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$"] [17592186345167 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$,  ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{b}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we get the system with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 7 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & \\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 25 & -25\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ 25{x}_{3} &=-25.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second   equation yields\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+3(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n Finally,  by substituting ${x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(-1)-(-1)=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1+(-1)+(-1)=-1.\n$$"] [17592186386599 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186386599 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -9\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -15\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3+5=8.$"] [17592186391903 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{{\\mathbf{b}}_{1},{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [17592186391903 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{x} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -8 & 2 & -4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -8 & 2 & -4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{